  "title": "How to Solve Simultaneous Equations (One Linear, One Quadratic)",
  "content": "<p>When one equation is linear and one is quadratic (e.g. a circle or parabola), use <strong>substitution</strong>.</p><p>1. Rearrange the linear equation for one variable</p><p>2. Substitute into the quadratic equation</p><p>3. Solve the resulting quadratic (you may get 0, 1, or 2 pairs of solutions)</p><p>4. Substitute back to find the corresponding values of the other variable</p><p><strong>Graphically:</strong> The solutions are where the line meets the curve. Two solutions = two intersection points, one solution = the line is tangent to the curve, no solutions = they don't meet.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Solve</strong> \\(y = x + 1\\) and \\(y = x^2 - 3x + 5\\)</p><p>Substitute: \\(x + 1 = x^2 - 3x + 5\\)</p><p>\\(0 = x^2 - 4x + 4 = (x - 2)^2\\)</p><p>\\(x = 2\\), \\(y = 3\\)</p>"
 },
